diagnosis:
Producers do not export what consumers need. The boundary 'no record type standing in for an object' (`cluster/cdk8s/AGENTS.md:8-13`) left a bare string constant as the only sanctioned carrier for a fact that crosses modules. Results:
- one port is written 6 times in one module (`ntfy.py:68` defines it; 220, 248, 252, 265-266 and 283 reuse it);
- `<cluster>-app` and `<name>-rw.<ns>.svc` are derived by hand 12 times (`terraform.py:33`, `atuin/user_provisioner.py:35-36`);
- the `external-creds-reader` literal appears 31 times;
- a consumer re-declares a producer's bearer name and key (`agentplane/staging.py:382, 389` against `ha_mcp.py:66-67`);
- `single_secret_store` returns a `str` (`external_secrets/single_secret_store.py:20-28`).
Only `Service(selector=deployment)` and Settings-derived env names hold by construction.

Cross-cutting pod policy was written against one dialect. `apply_pod_spec_patches` and `attract_to_zone` take a cdk8s-plus `Workload` (`pod_spec_patches.py:20-21`, `node_scheduling.py:13-14`), but 54 workloads are raw `k8s.Kube*`, and `SandboxTemplate` and `ScaledJob` embed their own pod types. Hardening is carried four ways:
- constructor keywords;
- `WRITABLE_ROOT` (`container_security.py:10-12`);
- the fluent-only patch;
- literal structs (`nix_cache/attic.py:266-279`, `agent_workspaces.py:91-113`).
Placement literals are spread wider still: 14 modules keep a local zone constant, `"hil-ovh"` appears 35 times, Helm values and raw CR dicts carry 26 `nodeSelector` keys in 17 modules, and the control-plane taint key is spelled in 33 modules.

Enforcement depends on the author remembering. Fleet rules are registered per chart by an explicit `add_fleet_rules` call (`fleet_rules.py:147-148`) on 12 of 191 charts, and `pod_hardening` does not walk `ScaledJob` (`fleet_rules.py:25-33`). Node facts are restated at each node:
- `SOPS_DECRYPTION` is passed by hand at 26 nodes, against 21 that derive it from `write_directory`'s SOPS siblings and 3 `sops_decryption` calls;
- 39 health-check entries are typed out (for example `dns_automation.py:87-94`), against 1 `flux.health_checks` call site, which feeds 2 nodes;
- 22 of 40 stateful Kustomizations lack the Orphan that `cluster/cdk8s/AGENTS.md:16-20` requires.
The graph check has the same gap: it reads `kustomize build` output, so nothing checks the 11 CRD edges known only from their comments, mostly for kinds a Helm chart renders, and `parse_cluster` reads only `kustomizations.k8s.yaml`, so haku-state-workloads is not a graph node at all (#7763).

Authorization is kept in sync by hand. Copying one Secret between namespaces has at least five mechanisms:
- the external-creds roster;
- `single_secret_store` per-pair stores built from the consumer's chart;
- app-owned ESO-ServiceAccount stores (`ntfy.py:82-112`);
- reflector mirrors;
- ClusterExternalSecret.
Store namespace allowlists are hand-kept copies of the consumer list (the outages are recorded in `TODO.md`; `external_secrets/config.py:95-118`). The committed YAML has three sources for the same `flux-system/external-creds-reader` ServiceAccount: two live Kustomizations (generated/dns-automation, k8s/github-secrets-sync-secrets) and the parked `k8s/parked/cloud-agent-tf`.

The Flux graph was built for readiness, so it grew tiny units and `wait` everywhere; its edges are mostly admission edges. On devel aaaa3a5b90 there are 171 Kustomizations and 249 `dependsOn` edges:
- 214 are admission edges: 184 to a CRD provider (11 of them known only from the comment beside the edge, mostly for kinds a Helm chart renders) and 30 to a `failurePolicy: Fail` webhook, 27 of them direct `kyverno` edges, which decision 13 would reclassify as runtime; none is to a `postBuild` source;
- the other 35 wait on runtime state: 27 on namespace units (#8185 removes them), 5 carry a #7094 rule-2 reason (`bootstrap-never-converges`, `destructive-if-out-of-order`), 2 order a user-provisioner Job after its app, and `artifact-generators` waits on `flux-system`;
- `wait` is true on 123 nodes, and 94 of them are unsuspended and gate nothing admission needs;
- 59 of 165 measurable units render 3 objects or fewer.
The owner is moving the graph to admission-only edges (#7094, and the merged #7441, #7458, #7495, #7541, #7545, #7559, #8176, #8181, #8183, #8186, #8187, #8188, #8190, #8191 and #8194) because the tiny units cost reconcile time and I/O, but the cdk8s layer still defaults `wait=True` (`flux.py:142`, since #7690) and nothing checks that an edge is justified: a rule-2 reason is a comment.

one_service:
`ntfy.py` (324 lines) is a typical small HTTP service. It uses:
- construct classes: `Deployment`, `Service`, `ServiceMonitor`, `ExternalSecret`;
- resource functions: `https_route`, `cnpg.cluster`;
- a mutator that writes a JSON patch: `apply_pod_spec_patches`;
- value factories: `http_probe`, `cnpg.same_owner_initdb`, and a module-local `_secret_env`;
- an opt-in validation: `fleet_rules.add_fleet_rules(chart)`;
- a Flux node that takes the `RenderedDirectory` the entry point's `write_directory` call returns (`ntfy.py:303-324`, `generate_manifests.py:744-752`).
The `PORT` constant is threaded by hand into 6 places, `_LABELS` into 6, and the UID `65532` is written 4 times. The route names its backend as the string `NAME`, not the Service it builds.

